title: Reverting a label
tags: Revert
kind: guide
summary: mf log revert <label> (or --op) writes the inverse onto the current state; plan shows it first, naming any later write it would overwrite.

```
mf -n music log revert plan 12          # what it would do — nothing written, no lock
mf -n music log revert 12               # revert label 12, its children included
mf -n music log revert --op 23          # only one of its operations (repeatable)
mf -n music log revert                  # the label of the newest operation
mf -n music log revert 12 --metadata-only
```

A metadata-only revert asks for nothing: it can itself be reverted, which is the point
of preferring it to a rollback.

''Changed since.'' Nothing blocks a revert: it goes back to what the operation replaced,
whatever was written there since ([[Reverting onto the current state]]). When a later
operation wrote the same cell, the plan names it, and the revert asks before overwriting
it (`--force` skips the question):

```
Changed since — the revert overwrites what was written there:
  op 23  set_field(rating)  on 5f2a1c88… — changed since by op 91 (rev 40, 2024-01-16 08:04)
```

''Files.'' When the plan needs file actions, the CLI shows it, asks (unless `--force`),
and carries them out under the navigation lock: moving a file back (`--on-move-available`,
default `apply`; `--on-move-unavailable`, default `ask`; an occupied destination goes to
the trash first, so no byte is lost), bringing content back from the trash, or sending
it back there. What it cannot do is simply left out of the revert, and named:

```
Reverted label 12 in label 57 (2 operations, 1 file moved).

  op 22  set_field(rating)    5 → 3
  op 23  file_moved(mfr_path) music/new.mp3 → music/old.mp3   (mv)
```

`--metadata-only` leaves out every operation needing a file action.

In the GUI, the [[log panel]]'s `log:revert` reverts what is selected ([[Log commands in the GUI]]).
